# Scenarios:

1. Booting into Safe Mode
Scenario: Your computer suddenly starts freezing after installing a new driver for your graphics card. Every time you try to log in, the screen goes black, and you can't access your desktop.

Solution:

Use Case: Boot into Safe Mode to troubleshoot the issue.
Action: Restart your computer in Safe Mode to load Windows with a minimal set of drivers. Once in Safe Mode, you can uninstall the problematic graphics driver and revert to the previous version.
2. Accessing UEFI/BIOS Settings
Scenario: You’ve just bought a new solid-state drive (SSD) and want to install Windows on it, but the drive isn’t showing up during the installation process.

Solution:

Use Case: Access UEFI/BIOS settings to change the boot order or enable the SSD.
Action: Restart your computer, go to UEFI Firmware Settings via Advanced Startup, and check if the SSD is detected. If necessary, change the boot order so that the SSD is the primary boot device, or enable any disabled ports.
3. Using System Restore
Scenario: After installing a new software application, your computer starts crashing frequently. The crashes happen even when you try to uninstall the software, and you're unsure what went wrong.

Solution:

Use Case: Use System Restore to revert your system to a stable state.
Action: Access System Restore through Advanced Startup and select a restore point from before you installed the problematic software. Your system will roll back to that point, removing the software and any changes it made.
4. Uninstalling Updates
Scenario: After a recent Windows update, your computer becomes sluggish, and some of your programs stop working correctly. You suspect the update might be causing the issues.

Solution:

Use Case: Uninstall the recent Windows update to resolve the problem.
Action: Use Advanced Startup to uninstall the latest update. This can help you return your system to its previous state before the update, potentially resolving the performance issues.
5. Command Prompt Access
Scenario: Your computer won’t boot into Windows, and you receive a "Boot Configuration Data (BCD) missing" error.

Solution:

Use Case: Use the Command Prompt to repair the boot configuration.
Action: Boot into Advanced Startup, open Command Prompt, and run commands like bootrec /fixmbr and bootrec /rebuildbcd to repair the boot files and restore your system’s ability to boot.
6. System Image Recovery
Scenario: Your system is infected with malware that’s severely damaging your files and applications. Even after trying to clean it up, the system remains unstable and problematic.

Solution:

Use Case: Restore your system from a previously created system image to remove all traces of malware and return to a stable state.
Action: If you have a system image backup, use Advanced Startup to initiate System Image Recovery. This will overwrite your current system with the backup, effectively removing the malware and restoring your system.
7. Startup Repair
Scenario: Your computer won’t boot up properly. It gets stuck on the Windows logo, and restarting the computer doesn't solve the issue.

Solution:

Use Case: Use Startup Repair to automatically diagnose and fix issues that prevent Windows from starting.
Action: Use Advanced Startup to run Startup Repair. Windows will scan your system for startup issues and attempt to fix them automatically, allowing your system to boot properly again.